==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|haven}}, {{m|enm|havene}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|hæfen||haven; harbour; port}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*habnō}}, {{m|gem-pro|*habanō}} (compare Dutch {{m|nl|haven}}, German {{m|de|Hafen}}, Danish/Norwegian {{m|da|havn}}), from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*habą||sea}} (compare Old English {{m|ang|hæf}}, Middle Low German {{m|gml|haf}}, Old Norse {{m|non|haf||sea}}, German {{m|de|Haff||bay or lagoon behind a spit}}, perhaps, in the sense of "heaving sea", etymologically identical with Old Norse {{m|non|haf||heaving, lifting, uplift, elevation}}, derived from {{cog|gem-pro|*habjaną||to lift, heave}}), or from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*kopno-}} (compare Irish {{m|ga|cúan}} 'harbor, recess, haven').

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈheɪvən/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-haven.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪvən|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[harbour]] or [[anchorage]] protected from the sea.
#* Shakespeare
#*: what shipping and what lading's in our '''haven'''
#* Tennyson
#*: their '''haven''' under the hill
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A place of [[safety]]; a refuge or sanctuary.
#* {{quote-journal
|date=December 21, 2011
|author=Helen Pidd
|title=Europeans migrate south as continent drifts deeper into crisis
|work=the Guardian
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/21/europe-migrants-crisis-irish-portuguese
|page=
|passage=Since its conception, the European Union has been a '''haven''' for those seeking refuge from war, persecution and poverty in other parts of the world.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A peaceful place.

